WEBVTT

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:28.270
<i>The year is 1889.</i>

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:33.860
<i>Jean is dismayed at being treated as a child by the Nautilus's crew.</i>

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:37.820
<i>He decides to build a helicopter by himself to prove he's no child.</i>

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:40.280
<i>As he works, he discovers his inexperience and</i>

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:43.910
<i>the need to rely on the knowledge possessed by the adult world.</i>

00:01:43.910 --> 00:01:48.330
<i>He recognizes he is still a child, and turns to Hanson for help.</i>

00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:49.790
<i>Jean then completes his helicopter and,</i>

00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:51.920
<i>together with Nadia, takes it into the skies.</i>

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:55.670
<i>Jean eagerly looks forward to the day when he can become a grown-up</i>

00:01:55.670 --> 00:01:58.760
<i>and make his dreams come true.</i>

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:33.250
It appears to be a cargo ship of British registry.

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:35.710
They don't seem to be aware of us.

00:02:35.710 --> 00:02:38.680
Maintain present position until it sails out of range.

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:39.430
Yes, Sir.

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:51.350
I guess we're safe.

00:02:51.350 --> 00:02:55.070
Yes. We haven't been this near land in a long time.

00:02:55.070 --> 00:03:00.780
That's true. I hope the landing party can find something useful.

00:03:29.270 --> 00:03:32.810
Say, we don't hear much about the sea monster these days.

00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:34.060
I wonder why?

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:39.030
I heard that the American fleet finished off the monster.

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:40.320
Is that true?

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:43.610
I guess so. They also say it was over two hundred meters long.

00:03:43.610 --> 00:03:48.080
What a huge monster! The American fleet has lots of power!

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:52.290
Now we can sail across the sea and feel safe.

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:57.420
Right. That ornery monster is sleeping with the fishes now!

00:03:59.630 --> 00:04:01.630
Probably talking about the Nautilus.

00:04:01.630 --> 00:04:03.180
I think so.

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:05.390
It's not a nice feeling.

00:04:05.390 --> 00:04:07.050
No, it's not.

00:04:07.510 --> 00:04:08.850
Yo!

00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:10.310
Join us.

00:04:11.270 --> 00:04:12.520
Find any clues to our objective?

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:16.770
Not a thing. It seems they've gone completely under cover.

00:04:17.150 --> 00:04:18.610
Just as I expected.

00:04:18.610 --> 00:04:21.110
These news reports are the last mention.

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:27.070
"The monster is sleeping with the fishes!"

00:04:27.740 --> 00:04:33.460
Recently, there's no information at all on the Garfish.

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:38.460
They've stopped attacking cargo ships in favor of some other evil plan.

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:40.380
Like making another Tower of Babel?

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:44.680
No, that plan was a failure. It must be something else.

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:46.550
What could it be?

00:04:46.550 --> 00:04:49.010
It's our job to find out.

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:52.890
What will Captain Nemo say if we return empty handed?

00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:56.190
I think we have to worry more about Miss Electra.

00:04:56.190 --> 00:04:59.110
That's true. We would be in real trouble.

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:04.110
Time for my report, Sir.

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:09.200
Ship's present position: West 25, North 306.

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:13.160
Depth 96 meters. Main engines operating smoothly.

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:17.420
Auxiliary engines, no problem. Food supplies adequate for 20 days.

00:05:17.790 --> 00:05:18.500
However...

00:05:19.130 --> 00:05:19.960
What is it?

00:05:20.250 --> 00:05:23.460
Three rounds of battle repairs

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:25.510
have left us short of maintenance materials.

00:05:26.590 --> 00:05:29.680
I believe it would be a good idea to return to base.

00:05:31.310 --> 00:05:34.350
We'll consider that after we have the report from the Chief Helmsman.

00:05:34.350 --> 00:05:34.890
Yes.

00:05:35.890 --> 00:05:40.860
So, we're apprentice engineers? We'll get our suits all dirty.

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:45.490
We've certainly been to a lot of different places.

00:05:45.490 --> 00:05:48.280
Europe must be far, far away by now.

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:51.580
I wonder where we are right now?

00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:55.290
No maps, no compass, I have no idea.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:56.790
I see.

00:05:57.210 --> 00:06:01.590
From the stars we saw last night, we must be way down south.

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:03.460
South, you say?

00:06:04.510 --> 00:06:08.180
I wonder where we're headed?

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:12.470
Attention all crew members.

00:06:12.470 --> 00:06:16.100
We will head for our main base to take on supplies and materials.

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:18.190
What's that?

00:06:29.910 --> 00:06:31.280
What is this?

00:06:31.780 --> 00:06:32.990
Arctic clothing.

00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:34.700
Arctic clothing?

00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:36.950
Yes, for protection against the arctic cold.

00:06:42.210 --> 00:06:46.300
I don't like it. Why do we have to wear such out of style clothing?

00:06:47.380 --> 00:06:49.220
It really is unstylish.

00:06:49.510 --> 00:06:54.680
I couldn't face Captain Nemo in clothes like this!

00:06:56.060 --> 00:06:57.140
What did you say?

00:06:58.100 --> 00:07:00.140
Is it starting again?

00:07:00.350 --> 00:07:03.520
But why do we have to wear clothes like this?

00:07:04.570 --> 00:07:06.110
Because we're going to the South Pole.

00:07:06.110 --> 00:07:07.530
What did you say?

00:07:08.610 --> 00:07:11.280
What's that? The South Pole?

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:14.450
What is Captain Nemo planning anyway?

00:07:14.450 --> 00:07:16.160
He wants to go back to his base.

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:17.290
His base?

00:07:17.290 --> 00:07:21.370
That place is just ice. How can he have a base in a place like that?

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:25.960
In the first place, no one even knows what kind of place it is.

00:07:25.960 --> 00:07:28.340
Is the South Pole truly a terrible place?

00:07:29.260 --> 00:07:34.300
It sure is! No ship has even been near the South Pole!

00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:37.220
Terrible winds...

00:07:38.270 --> 00:07:40.230
Thick ice blocks...

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:45.020
And it's so cold that tears freeze on your face.

00:07:45.020 --> 00:07:47.780
It's no place for humans!

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:51.150
It's that bad?

00:07:51.150 --> 00:07:54.910
Many people have tried to reach the South Pole,

00:07:54.910 --> 00:07:57.120
and they all failed!!

00:07:57.620 --> 00:08:00.410
I don't care how wonderful this ship is,

00:08:00.410 --> 00:08:03.040
it just cannot survive at the South Pole!

00:08:03.500 --> 00:08:05.210
Jean, is that true?

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:09.340
I'm sure we'll be all right.

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:13.550
Gosh, the South Pole.

00:08:13.930 --> 00:08:17.510
I wonder what kind of a place it is? What an opportunity!

00:08:17.970 --> 00:08:18.930
The South Pole?

00:08:19.270 --> 00:08:22.640
You've been there, haven't you? What kind of a place is it?

00:08:25.810 --> 00:08:29.150
That's right. That's where our base is.

00:08:31.900 --> 00:08:34.030
What kind of place is it?

00:08:34.030 --> 00:08:37.740
Let's see, that's where our base is located.

00:08:44.040 --> 00:08:47.000
Well, that's where our base is located.

00:08:47.330 --> 00:08:48.960
That's where our base is.

00:08:49.170 --> 00:08:50.670
Our base is located there.

00:08:53.130 --> 00:08:54.130
The South Pole?

00:08:54.130 --> 00:08:56.090
Yes, what kind of place is it?

00:08:57.220 --> 00:09:00.060
That's where the base for the Nautilus is located.

00:09:02.020 --> 00:09:02.850
What's the matter?

00:09:03.310 --> 00:09:06.060
That's what everybody says when we ask!

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:11.530
I see. I think everyone wants to surprise you.

00:09:12.820 --> 00:09:15.240
All right, I'll show you.

00:09:15.240 --> 00:09:17.200
Hurray!

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:22.330
It's like magic!

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:25.790
Not quite. It's only the power of science.

00:09:25.790 --> 00:09:30.670
This is amazing! I wonder how it works? Like a magic lantern?

00:09:30.670 --> 00:09:32.670
An image created by an electronic brain

00:09:32.670 --> 00:09:36.180
is projected by electricity, magnets and electronics.

00:09:37.380 --> 00:09:39.680
What is an electronic brain?

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:43.470
I guess you could call it a machine that can think.

00:09:43.470 --> 00:09:46.100
I'll explain the details later.

00:09:46.100 --> 00:09:48.060
Right now you're interested in the South Pole, right?

00:09:48.060 --> 00:09:49.150
Yes.

00:09:51.900 --> 00:09:54.070
This is the Antarctic Continent.

00:09:55.360 --> 00:09:58.780
Amazing! This is a drawing of the shoreline.

00:09:58.780 --> 00:10:00.450
What's so terrific about it?

00:10:00.450 --> 00:10:03.370
The Antarctic shoreline is always covered with ice.

00:10:03.370 --> 00:10:06.460
No one knows exactly where it lies.

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:09.420
But a map showing it exists on this ship.

00:10:10.790 --> 00:10:13.000
It's unbelievable!

00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:14.880
Tell me how you drew the shoreline.

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:17.380
Actually, we were not the ones who mapped it.

00:10:17.380 --> 00:10:20.340
The data was already contained in the electronic data bank.

00:10:20.340 --> 00:10:22.390
How did they find out?

00:10:23.060 --> 00:10:24.140
Well, that's...

00:10:24.680 --> 00:10:25.350
That's?

00:10:26.230 --> 00:10:28.060
A secret.

00:10:28.730 --> 00:10:30.860
Geez, that's not fair!

00:10:31.730 --> 00:10:33.860
Say, what is this stamp?

00:10:33.860 --> 00:10:35.820
That's the site of our secret base.

00:10:35.820 --> 00:10:38.780
But that's in the middle of the continent.

00:10:38.780 --> 00:10:41.910
How can a ship get there? Do we walk?

00:10:43.620 --> 00:10:45.700
Don't worry. This ship will take us there.

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:48.040
But how?

00:10:48.750 --> 00:10:49.580
That's...

00:10:50.120 --> 00:10:50.790
That's?

00:10:51.420 --> 00:10:53.250
A secret.

00:10:53.250 --> 00:10:54.960
Geez! Unfair!

00:10:58.420 --> 00:11:00.300
Jean, you jerk!

00:11:18.360 --> 00:11:20.700
No target within a radius of twenty kilometers.

00:11:20.700 --> 00:11:22.030
No electronic jamming.

00:11:22.030 --> 00:11:24.990
Switching on the air compressor.

00:11:24.990 --> 00:11:27.080
We'll be entering the Antarctic Zone shortly.

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:35.590
We'll reach the South Pole soon.

00:11:36.130 --> 00:11:39.510
I sure hope this ship can take us there.

00:11:42.010 --> 00:11:43.470
What's going on?

00:11:46.430 --> 00:11:49.100
We're running into some ice floes. They're still small ones.

00:11:55.560 --> 00:11:57.520
Obstacle lying directly in front.

00:12:03.070 --> 00:12:06.030
Ice floes, about five meters in thickness.

00:12:06.660 --> 00:12:07.990
What shall we do?

00:12:07.990 --> 00:12:09.040
The compressed air?

00:12:09.500 --> 00:12:12.370
Full up to cylinder 48.

00:12:12.370 --> 00:12:13.870
It will take two minutes to fill the remaining two.

00:12:14.120 --> 00:12:16.170
Upon completion, prepare to submerge.

00:12:16.170 --> 00:12:16.710
Yes, Sir.

00:12:17.130 --> 00:12:21.840
Geez, we've reached the South Pole, and they tell us we can't go out!

00:12:21.840 --> 00:12:24.430
At least we won't catch cold.

00:12:25.010 --> 00:12:26.800
But I want to see.

00:12:28.760 --> 00:12:30.270
All crew, prepare to submerge.

00:12:30.520 --> 00:12:32.350
Each man to his work station.

00:12:32.350 --> 00:12:35.060
I'm going up to the bridge.

00:12:46.740 --> 00:12:48.660
Diving system operating satisfactorily.

00:12:48.910 --> 00:12:51.700
Depth 60 meters. Ballast normal.

00:13:02.340 --> 00:13:04.930
A huge iceberg ahead between 11 and 2 o'clock.

00:13:05.470 --> 00:13:07.010
Turn thirty degrees to starboard.

00:13:07.010 --> 00:13:07.760
Aye, Sir.

00:13:14.890 --> 00:13:17.230
Gee, it's huge!

00:13:21.940 --> 00:13:24.400
Depth 80 meters. Maintaining present heading.

00:13:30.700 --> 00:13:34.580
This is strange. The water temperature is over 7 degrees!

00:13:34.580 --> 00:13:36.120
Captain, the sea bed!

00:13:39.750 --> 00:13:41.920
What is all this?

00:13:41.920 --> 00:13:43.460
What has happened here?

00:13:44.010 --> 00:13:48.550
This is terrible! It's just like a graveyard for fish.

00:13:49.260 --> 00:13:52.180
First Officer, check the composition of the sea water.

00:13:52.180 --> 00:13:52.600
Yes, Sir.

00:13:57.640 --> 00:13:58.980
The magnetic field is unstable.

00:13:58.980 --> 00:14:01.730
Water density is plus point seven five.

00:14:01.730 --> 00:14:04.690
The presence of carbon dioxide, hydrogen sulfide,

00:14:05.360 --> 00:14:07.320
and heavy metal ions is two hundred times normal!

00:14:07.320 --> 00:14:08.780
Just as I thought.

00:14:15.200 --> 00:14:16.790
What's going on?

00:14:16.790 --> 00:14:18.000
An undersea volcano!

00:14:21.960 --> 00:14:23.130
Incredible!

00:14:23.130 --> 00:14:25.630
I thought all the volcanoes around here were extinct.

00:14:25.630 --> 00:14:28.930
Mother Nature always has surprises for us, doesn't she?

00:14:28.930 --> 00:14:29.800
She certainly does.

00:14:35.600 --> 00:14:37.140
What shall we do?

00:14:37.140 --> 00:14:39.230
It can't be helped. Go around it.

00:14:39.230 --> 00:14:39.690
Aye, Sir.

00:14:47.360 --> 00:14:49.490
What was all that commotion a while back?

00:14:49.490 --> 00:14:52.240
I heard we ran into an undersea volcano.

00:14:52.240 --> 00:14:53.870
A volcano?

00:14:54.160 --> 00:14:57.450
You mean to tell me there are volcanoes under the sea?

00:14:58.750 --> 00:15:01.330
The South Pole seems to be quite a dangerous place.

00:15:06.340 --> 00:15:09.340
We are passing under an ice sheet. Depth 160 meters.

00:15:11.510 --> 00:15:15.430
Unidentified object approaching from 11 o'clock.

00:15:15.430 --> 00:15:18.140
Relative approach speed 60 knots.

00:15:18.140 --> 00:15:19.890
It can't be a Garfish!

00:15:19.890 --> 00:15:21.980
You have ten seconds to identify it.

00:15:21.980 --> 00:15:22.730
Yes, Sir!

00:15:27.030 --> 00:15:29.030
No mechanical propulsion noise detected.

00:15:29.030 --> 00:15:32.870
No magnetic reaction. So, it can't be a Garfish.

00:15:32.870 --> 00:15:34.370
Then what is it?

00:15:34.370 --> 00:15:36.540
I have no idea!

00:15:36.540 --> 00:15:37.870
What shall we do?

00:15:37.870 --> 00:15:39.330
Captain Nemo...

00:15:40.710 --> 00:15:43.210
I am declaring General Quarters.

00:15:43.210 --> 00:15:46.630
Yes, Sir! All hands to general quarters!

00:15:46.630 --> 00:15:49.510
Repeat, all hands to general quarters!

00:15:49.510 --> 00:15:55.260
This time it's war? The South Pole is really a dangerous place.

00:15:58.310 --> 00:16:00.850
It's coming! It's latched on to our position.

00:16:05.690 --> 00:16:06.940
Six hundred meters to contact.

00:16:06.940 --> 00:16:09.070
Vertical dive, now!

00:16:17.410 --> 00:16:19.160
Did you see that?

00:16:19.160 --> 00:16:19.660
I sure did!

00:16:19.660 --> 00:16:21.540
That was no submarine!

00:16:22.040 --> 00:16:23.000
Something alive?

00:16:23.250 --> 00:16:25.460
It looked like a giant squid!

00:16:25.830 --> 00:16:29.550
Ice on top of us and a giant squid below.

00:16:29.550 --> 00:16:31.300
This is dangerous!

00:16:47.270 --> 00:16:49.110
That thing brushed us!

00:16:51.570 --> 00:16:56.030
It's alive! Maybe it thinks we are invading its territory.

00:17:02.250 --> 00:17:04.210
It's coming back! Starboard bow!

00:17:04.210 --> 00:17:05.830
Damn! Get us away!

00:17:05.830 --> 00:17:08.380
Engines at full speed! Hard to port!

00:17:12.510 --> 00:17:14.470
No good! It's going to ram us head on!

00:17:14.470 --> 00:17:16.340
All crew members, prepare for collision!

00:17:23.100 --> 00:17:24.890
What's going on?

00:17:37.450 --> 00:17:38.370
I can't believe it!

00:17:38.660 --> 00:17:39.780
It's a giant shellfish.

00:17:39.780 --> 00:17:42.410
They existed hundreds of millions of years ago!

00:17:42.410 --> 00:17:43.500
And it's alive today?

00:17:48.130 --> 00:17:49.290
That thing is crushing us!

00:17:49.670 --> 00:17:51.630
It's impossible to use our weapons.

00:17:51.630 --> 00:17:54.510
Not with it clinging to the ship.

00:17:56.260 --> 00:17:58.680
It's even cracking our reinforced tectite!

00:17:58.680 --> 00:18:00.350
Close the armored shutters.

00:18:00.350 --> 00:18:00.760
Yes, Sir!

00:18:08.770 --> 00:18:09.900
Damn!

00:18:10.270 --> 00:18:11.690
I've got it!

00:18:11.690 --> 00:18:15.360
Captain, what if we sent a high voltage current through the hull?

00:18:15.860 --> 00:18:17.570
Good idea!

00:18:19.200 --> 00:18:20.780
Okay, let's try it, First Officer.

00:18:21.370 --> 00:18:22.740
Yes, Sir!

00:18:40.220 --> 00:18:44.510
Nautilus the submarine versus Nautilus the shellfish!

00:18:44.510 --> 00:18:45.810
What a mess!

00:18:48.310 --> 00:18:49.480
Raise the voltage!

00:18:49.480 --> 00:18:49.980
Yes, Sir!

00:18:58.780 --> 00:18:59.610
It won't let go!

00:19:02.910 --> 00:19:05.240
Engines at full power! Begin vertical climb!

00:19:05.240 --> 00:19:06.700
Sir, there's an iceberg on top of us!

00:19:06.910 --> 00:19:09.040
I know! We'll ram it and the shellfish!

00:19:09.040 --> 00:19:10.210
Aye, aye!

00:19:17.630 --> 00:19:19.380
What's going on?

00:19:25.760 --> 00:19:28.100
It's still clinging to us.

00:19:28.100 --> 00:19:30.100
It's worse than a Garfish.

00:19:33.310 --> 00:19:35.360
The bridge can't take much more of this!

00:19:41.240 --> 00:19:43.700
Hard to starboard! Heading 107.

00:19:43.700 --> 00:19:45.990
But that's back toward the volcano!

00:19:46.370 --> 00:19:48.160
Doesn't matter, 107.

00:19:48.790 --> 00:19:49.580
Yes, Sir!

00:20:12.480 --> 00:20:13.560
Incredible!

00:20:15.610 --> 00:20:17.900
Don't worry, proceed straight into the crater!

00:20:17.900 --> 00:20:20.860
But the heat will destroy the Nautilus!

00:20:23.200 --> 00:20:26.700
There's no other way. Let's do it!

00:20:28.030 --> 00:20:29.580
Right!

00:20:31.000 --> 00:20:33.710
Purge all compressed air through tank 30!

00:20:39.460 --> 00:20:40.630
Purging is complete!

00:20:40.630 --> 00:20:42.010
Here we go!

00:20:52.850 --> 00:20:53.690
The shellfish?

00:20:53.690 --> 00:20:54.900
Still alive.

00:20:58.230 --> 00:21:01.190
Chief Helmsman, pass as close as you can to the crater.

00:21:01.400 --> 00:21:02.400
Aye, Sir!

00:21:07.030 --> 00:21:08.660
It's a battle of nerves.

00:21:08.990 --> 00:21:11.910
Outside temperature, 1200 degrees. Internal temperature, 40!

00:21:12.620 --> 00:21:14.870
It certainly is hot!

00:21:15.250 --> 00:21:17.750
The South Pole sure is a funny place.

00:21:23.670 --> 00:21:26.300
How can it stay alive in this heat?

00:21:26.300 --> 00:21:27.760
It's a monster!

00:21:33.560 --> 00:21:35.310
The bridge is reaching its limit!

00:21:35.560 --> 00:21:36.520
Captain...

00:21:36.900 --> 00:21:39.980
It's alive. It has to let go.

00:21:49.160 --> 00:21:51.030
We did it!

00:22:03.260 --> 00:22:04.710
We were lucky.

00:22:04.710 --> 00:22:08.640
I see! It was alive and couldn't breathe inside the crater.

00:22:08.640 --> 00:22:10.180
So it had to let go.

00:22:10.430 --> 00:22:13.560
It's amazing to think that a creature like that still exists.

00:22:13.560 --> 00:22:17.140
Nature has many things beyond the knowledge of mankind.

00:22:24.440 --> 00:22:26.990
Set bearing 110. Let's head for our base.

00:22:26.990 --> 00:22:27.530
Yes, Sir.

00:22:37.500 --> 00:22:41.630
It's amazing to find a tunnel like this at the bottom of the sea!

00:22:41.630 --> 00:22:43.840
It's unknown to mankind.

00:22:55.520 --> 00:22:59.230
We are passing out of the tunnel and into the sea.

00:23:15.120 --> 00:23:16.290
This is unbelievable!

00:23:16.290 --> 00:23:19.330
We should be under a continent, but we're in this huge sea!

00:23:23.170 --> 00:23:25.300
Jean, this is the place on the map.

00:23:25.300 --> 00:23:26.800
This place?

00:23:29.760 --> 00:23:32.140
Yes, a huge undersea cavern.
